course: dictionary
lesson: 16085
title: Warrant: a paper, a reason, a need
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Warrant» is a formal word, so it often shows up in stories about police, judges and news. As a noun, a warrant is an official paper from a judge. It lets the police do something, such as search a house. «The police showed a warrant before they searched the house.» The word «before» and the words about searching are your clues that it is a legal paper.
explain: «Warrant» can also be a verb, meaning to make something right or necessary. «The strange noise warrants a closer look.» Ask yourself: what would fit in place of «warrants»? You could say «needs» or «deserves», so the noise needs a closer look.
explain: Warrant can also mean a good reason that makes something fair. «Losing the game is no warrant for shouting at your friends.» Losing might make you cross, but it does not make shouting fair. Try swapping in «reason» and see if the sentence still makes sense: «Losing is no reason for shouting» works, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A warrant is an official paper, signed by a judge, that lets the police do something, such as search a house or arrest someone.
fact: As a verb, «warrant» means to make something right or necessary.
fact: «The problem warrants a closer look.» means the problem needs a closer look.
fact: Warrant can also mean a good reason that makes something fair.
fact: Formal words like «warrant» are often used in serious writing, news reports and exams, so it helps to know them.
exercise: «The police showed a warrant from the judge before they searched the house.» What is a warrant here? | A prize for the best detective | *An official paper that lets the police search a house | A kind of dog that finds lost things | A loud siren on a police car
exercise: «The strange noise warrants a closer look.» What does warrants mean? | *Makes a closer look necessary | Stops the noise from happening | Hides the noise away | Makes the noise quieter
exercise: Ella says, «There is no warrant for shouting at your friends.» What does warrant mean here? | *A reason that makes something fair | A note from the teacher | A long, thin piece of wood | A kind of sweet
test: «The judge signed an arrest warrant for the thief.» What does warrant mean? | A loud shout for help | A warm kind of coat | A reward for finding a lost bag | *An official paper that lets the police arrest someone
test: «Does the evidence warrant this conclusion?» In plain words, this asks: | Is the picture pretty? | *Is it enough reason? | Was the report written in pencil? | Is the story too long to read?
exam: «The broken fence warrants a new gate.» What does warrants mean? | *Makes a new gate necessary | Hides the gate behind a tree | Takes the gate away for good | Paints the fence blue
